ion of Acute Sinusitis", "posterior": 0.288, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 2, "disconfirming": 2, "last_disconfirming_digest": "Lack of classic bacterial superinfection features weakly challenges B2.1 but provides neutral/weak support for mucocele…"}, "action_intents": [{"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "confirm"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B2.2", "B2.3", "B1"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=75%, source=both]\n\nBacterial Superinfection of Acute Sinusitis vs Mucocele with Secondary Infection:\n  Favours first:  bad breath, cough, ear pressure, facial pain or pressure, fatigue\n  Favours second: increased exudation, pain that worsens over time, purulent wound secretion, pus or fluid leakage, red skin around the injury\n\nBacterial Superinfection of Acute Sinusitis vs Orbital Cellulitis:\n  Favours first:  bad breath, cough, ear pressure, facial pain or pressure, fatigue\n  Favours second: abnormality of the face, agenesis of lateral incisor, autosomal dominant inheritance, bilateral cleft lip, bilateral cleft palate\n\nMucocele with Secondary Infection vs Orbital Cellulitis:\n  Favours first:  increased exudation, pain that worsens over time, purulent wound secretion, pus or fluid leakage, red skin around the injury\n  Favours second: abnormality of the face, agenesis of lateral incisor, autosomal dominant inheritance, bilateral cleft lip, bilateral cleft palate\n\nRelated to Orbital Cellulitis: acute orbital inflammation, cellulitis (disease)\n\n[RAG context for 'A 7-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department by his parents for worsening symptoms of previously diagnosed acute sinusitis' (score=0.64):]\n  Source: Clinical practice guideline (update): adult sinusitis. > History and Physical\n  Children show slight differences in the clinical signs of acute bacterial rhinosinusitis. Besides the 10-day duration, key symptoms, and double worsening, children are more prone to have fevers. Initi\n[RAG context for 'A 7-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department by his parents for worsening symptoms of previously diagnosed acute sinusitis' (score=0.68):]\n  Source: Pediatrics_Nelson\n  Obstruction to mucociliary flow, such as mucosal edemaresulting from the common cold, impedes sinus drainage andpredisposes to bacterial proliferation. In 90% of children withacute sinusitis, the bact\n[RAG context for 'Initially' (score=0.61):]\n  Source: InternalMed_Harrison\n  S. aureus (MRSA) as an emerging cause. It is difficult to assess whether a cultured bacterium represents a true infecting organism, an insufficiently deep sample (which would not be expected to be ste\n[RAG context for 'Initially' (score=0.65):]\n  Source: First_Aid_Step2\n  Peritonsillar abscess may present with odynophagia, trismus (“lockjaw”), a muffed voice, unilateral tonsillar enlargement, and erythema, with the uvula and soft palate deviated away from the affected \n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  worsening symptoms of previously diagnosed acute sinusitis despite initial decongestant treatment → treatment failure in acute sinusitis → Bacterial Superinfection of Acute Sinusitis\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Evaluate for bacterial superinfection given treatment failure with purulent nasal discharge and persistent symptoms despite initial therapy\n  nasal discharge with worsening pain in a child with history of asthma → progression to orbital cellulitis → Orbital Cellulitis\n    (frequency: rare)\n    Suggestion: Assess for orbital signs (periorbital swelling, eye movement restriction) given the risk of complications in asthmatic children\n  worsening pain and nasal discharge after initial treatment → mucocele formation with secondary infection → Mucocele with Secondary Infection\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Sugg